.TH CCOL_DYNMQ_TRY_RECV_ZC 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc \- try to receive from a dynamic queue with no block
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc(ccol_dynamic_queue *dq,
                                 c_message_t *target_buf);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function tries to remove the message at the head of the dynamic queue
and to give it back in target_buf. If the queue is empty, the call returns
ccol_container_empty immediately. It never waits for a message.

On success, the ownership of the data pointer of the message moves to the
caller. target_buf->data is the pointer that the sender gave, and
target_buf->size is its size in bytes. The caller is the one that frees the
data after it finishes with it. The library copies no bytes. Only the
pointer and its size move. A sender can put a message into the queue with a
NULL data pointer and a size of zero. Such a message arrives in the same
way. target_buf->data can therefore legitimately be NULL with
target_buf->size at 0. Check it before you dereference it.

On every failure, the function does not change target_buf, and the queue
keeps everything that it held.

Use this receive call immediately after a callback reports that a dynamic
queue selectable is ready. That callback comes from ccol_select(),
ccol_select_timed(), or an ccol_event_loop. Those report readiness only,
and they never take the message themselves. Another consumer can take the
message first. ccol_container_empty covers exactly that case.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq          Dynamic queue to receive from
target_buf  Buffer that gets the message. It also gets the ownership of
            the data.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success         - The function received a message. The caller owns
                       target_buf->data and must free it.
ccol_invalid_args    - dq or target_buf is NULL
ccol_container_empty - The queue is empty. The function does not change
                       target_buf.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function never blocks.
The function succeeds while sends on the queue are off. A turn off of
sends only stops new messages from entering the queue. A receive still
gets the messages that the queue already holds.
A receive that succeeds wakes one write-direction waiter on this queue.
Such a waiter is a ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call, or an
ccol_event_loop registration.
The function is thread-safe.
.fi
